    pr_ref:  # d84c
    description: "Trackerized 2026-11-14 at the owner's request (\"trackerize the third row but do not act on it just yet\"). STATUS needs_human_review ON PURPOSE: do not start until the owner says go.\n\nWHAT IS ASKED. Narrow the import graph that smart-test's import-closure union walks (hypergumbo_core/import_closure.py, shipped in PR #2186 for INV-mikag). Today every import statement anywhere in any file is an edge, including an import inside a function body. The change: an import inside a function body is an edge only when the importing file is OUTSIDE the source roots (a test file, a conftest.py, a test helper). For a file under packages/<pkg>/src, only imports that run when the file is imported are edges: module level, class body, under try or if at those levels. Everything else stays as shipped (the packages above an imported module, literal import_module and __import__, the conftest.py files above a test, sibling helpers, the TYPE_CHECKING exclusion, the trigger: a changed module or class body, or a deleted file).\n\nWHY. The shipped rule costs more than the figure the owner ruled on. Replayed with the merged module over the 90 PRs that changed package source between 2026-10-30 and 2026-11-14, selected test time rises 25.3 percent (48 PRs grow; median share of the suite 52 to 68 percent); the estimate before the ruling was 18 percent. Cause: the root conftest.py imports hypergumbo_core.analyze.registry inside a fixture; the graph counts the package above an imported module, so every test file reaches hypergumbo_core/__init__.py, which imports ir at module level; ir imports catalog inside a function and catalog imports cli inside a function. So every file in the import closure of cli is loaded by all 1414 test files, and an import-time change to one selects the whole suite as a targeted run (the INV-mikag repro, one constant in schema.py: 1414 test files selected, 1162 of them by the closure, 252 before the fix).\n\nMEASURED FOR THIS VARIANT (same replay, same 90 PRs; ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/mikag_cost_11142026/after_merge/variants.py, row no-lazy-chain in variants_out.txt):\n- selected test time +9.8 percent (232518 to 255232 test seconds); 41 of 90 PRs grow; median share of the suite 52 to 56 percent;\n- the closure of schema.py is 312 test files (1414 shipped) and still holds packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_stable_shape_ids.py, the test of the INV-mikag repro.\nFor comparison, same replay: shipped +25.3 percent; conftest chain not counted +17.8; module-level imports only, everywhere, +5.3.\nThe variant was measured by a lab script that swaps the collector class per file (a collector that does not descend into FunctionDef, AsyncFunctionDef or Lambda, used for files where import_closure.module_name(path) is not None). It was NOT run through smart-test or the bed.\n\nWHAT IT GIVES UP. This takes back part of the owner ruling of 2026-11-14 (module-level AND function-level imports): a function-level import in a SOURCE module stops being an edge. A test that reaches the changed file only through such an import is no longer selected by the closure. The bed has the shape: bedcore/reads_lazily.py imports limits inside a function, and edit constant-read-elsewhere has test_reads_lazily.py as MUST-select. Under this variant the closure does not add it (expected from the rule; not run), so tests/test_smart_test_bed.py, which asserts all four readers are in the manifest, changes with it and the bed then scores one dropped MUST for that edit. How many real test files are in that position was not measured; the size of the cut is the 1414 to 312 above. The twice-daily full suite is what catches such a break.\n\nSCOPE OF THE CHANGE.\n- import_closure.py: the collector takes whether the file is a source file; module docstring (WHICH IMPORTS COUNT, NOT COUNTED, COST) rewritten for the new rule.\n- packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_import_closure.py: the lazy-import case splits in two (in a test file: an edge; in a source module: not an edge); a case for the chain conftest fixture -> package __init__ -> module-level import, which must stay.\n- Bed: constant-read-elsewhere and its assertions in tests/test_smart_test_bed.py; a reader that imports lazily from a TEST file, so the half that stays is pinned too.\n- docs/adr/0010-modular-packages-and-smart-testing.md, the import-closure paragraph and its bullets: the rule and the cost figures. ADR-0010 and the module docstring today state 18 percent, labelled as measured before the build; that figure is wrong for what shipped (25.3) and is replaced here by the figure measured for the rule this item ships.\n- CHANGELOG line.\n\nDONE WHEN. (1) The INV-mikag repro still passes: STABLE_ID_SCHEME edited in schema.py, scripts/smart-test --manifest, the manifest holds test_stable_shape_ids.py. (2) The same manifest is smaller than the 1414 files the shipped rule selects; report the number. (3) The replay over the 90 PRs is re-run with the merged module (mikag_cost_11142026/real_cost.py) and its figure is the one in the docs. (4) The bed and unit tests above pass with hypergumbo 9.0.0 slicing and with the tree's own build slicing (CI has no pipx build), and a mutation control that restores function-level edges for source modules turns the new unit case red. (5) The dropped MUST for the lazy source reader is stated in ADR-0010 as a known miss, not hidden.\n\nRELATED. INV-mikag-zubud-mujar-vomuk-kapir-pojim-fahok-hulop (satisfied; this narrows its fix and its closure note records the cost). INV-vimin-kasor-romav-dupub-godul-zubaj-mazit-zovor (a changed function body called at import; separate gap, unaffected). The owner is also weighing a faster, less certain PR selection backstopped by the full-suite cron; if that goes ahead it may replace this."  # d84c
